\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.6.1}Frequency Stabilisation}{27}{subsection.3.6.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces PDH optical and control schematic. Green optical path represents the carrier frequency, blue and orange lines represent the upper and lower sidebands respectively, generated by an EOM. The reflected light is directed onto a fast photodiode, and the signal is demodulated with the same oscillator signal used to drive the EOM, but delayed by some chosen phase. The signal is finally low pass filtered to recover the error signal, an example of which is seen in the upper right panel for a finesse $F\sim 4000$, a free spectral range of $FSR = 1.5~\unit {\giga \hertz }$, and a modulation frequency of $f_{mod} = 5~\unit {\mega \hertz }$. }}{28}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:PDH}{{3.8}{28}{PDH optical and control schematic. Green optical path represents the carrier frequency, blue and orange lines represent the upper and lower sidebands respectively, generated by an EOM. The reflected light is directed onto a fast photodiode, and the signal is demodulated with the same oscillator signal used to drive the EOM, but delayed by some chosen phase. The signal is finally low pass filtered to recover the error signal, an example of which is seen in the upper right panel for a finesse $F\sim 4000$, a free spectral range of $FSR = 1.5~\unit {\giga \hertz }$, and a modulation frequency of $f_{mod} = 5~\unit {\mega \hertz }$}{figure.caption.9}{}}
